Z||000000||DaveL||03-27-2001||08:49 AM||||Coming home from work the other day, someone jammed on their brakes in front of me, so I jammed on mine too. The lady driving the 83 Diplomat slammed into the back of mt 87 SE. When I got out, I expected the rear end to have been cleaned off complately. To my surprise the damage wasn't all that bad, and I drove away. Pretty surprising! Estimate 1500 to repair. Sound reasonable? I didn't think it'd really be that high...||||reg||1
Z||000001||TheFiero64||03-27-2001||12:31 PM||||2 years ago I had a deer run into my Fiero and it just took the headlight motor with it and cracked my fender. It was $1200 worth of damage. took $25 to fix. I love having a parts car. I got $700 after my deductible. I think I made out great.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||10
Z||000002||tgowens||03-27-2001||12:35 PM||||you might look under the body panels though. A hit from the rear can "warp" the frame all the way through to the front. Make sure your door is straight (not the door skin but the door itself under the skin) and also remove the rear fascia and take a good look, i'll bet you'll find more damage than you see from the outside. Remember, the Enduroflex body panels are fairly forgiving but the metal underneath, well, that's a different story....it's not just what the eye can see from the outside..........||||reg||1
